00:11:43.410 --> 00:11:45.099
I'm a fan of Jared Hess.

00:11:45.099 --> 00:11:46.797
They weren't as into it as I was.

00:11:46.797 --> 00:11:50.480
"Don Verdean, Biblical Archaeologist."

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:52.850
Well I think the thing
we like about Jared Hess

00:11:52.850 --> 00:11:54.483
is that he,

00:11:56.484 --> 00:11:58.703
if we were to make movies,

00:11:59.710 --> 00:12:01.880
we've always thought,
especially because at that time,

00:12:01.880 --> 00:12:03.750
like 2004 when that one came out,

00:12:03.750 --> 00:12:04.830
we were thinking, we
were trying to figure out

00:12:04.830 --> 00:12:06.160
what is this career gonna look like,

00:12:06.160 --> 00:12:08.040
and where is this eventually going to go.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:09.180
And I think we always assumed

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:10.810
that it would end at making movies.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:12.700
And maybe it will Link,
maybe it will one day.

00:12:12.700 --> 00:12:13.835
Right.

00:12:13.835 --> 00:12:16.260
But we had this vision that, oh,

00:12:16.260 --> 00:12:18.640
the way that he's kind of drawing on

00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:21.477
the weird, quirky stuff from his life,

00:12:21.477 --> 00:12:24.390
and his childhood, and the way
that he puts that on screen,

00:12:24.390 --> 00:12:26.620
in very specific ways,
it's just how we imagined

00:12:26.620 --> 00:12:28.626
that we would always make comedy.

00:12:28.626 --> 00:12:29.459
It was a different type of comedy.

00:12:29.459 --> 00:12:31.760
That's why it had to be
on my list, I felt like,

00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:35.250
because it was so inspiring
for me, and for both of us,

00:12:35.250 --> 00:12:38.330
because it was this
specific strain of comedy

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:40.320
that we had never experienced,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:45.280
that every choice was a comedic choice.

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.040
And it came together
in a way that was like

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.950
it was so odd that it became
cool in spite of itself.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:56.950
Even the music cues and like
the composition, everything,

00:12:57.410 --> 00:13:00.450
was just delightfully strange,

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:03.180
and I think it gave us confidence that

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:06.300
those instincts, comedic
instincts that we had

00:13:06.300 --> 00:13:10.220
that were kind of kindred
to his was something

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:13.370
that people could dig.

00:13:13.370 --> 00:13:18.370
And it gave us more confidence
to explore our comedic voice.

00:13:20.690 --> 00:13:22.560
So that's why I had to
make the list for me.

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:25.640
Okay, I'll pick up with
number nine in one second.

00:13:25.640 --> 00:13:28.040
First, we do want to take a short break,

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:31.353
let you know that we've
got pop sockets, people.

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:37.820
At mythical.com, you can
go and pick up pop sockets.

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:39.640
We've got the mythical pop socket,

00:13:39.640 --> 00:13:41.420
we've got the GMM pop socket.

00:13:41.420 --> 00:13:42.585
I'm gonna put it up--

00:13:42.585 --> 00:13:43.540
You can prop up your phone.

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:44.410
I'm gonna make it pop.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:46.620
Now, we don't have it
on our phones right now

00:13:46.620 --> 00:13:48.990
because we're going to sell these.

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:49.893
Check this out.

00:13:52.574 --> 00:13:53.407
I mean.

00:13:53.407 --> 00:13:54.410
It's very satisfying.

00:13:54.410 --> 00:13:57.460
The thing about a pop socket
is that it's basically,

00:13:57.460 --> 00:14:00.623
it's still the only acceptable
form of a fidget toy,

00:14:01.760 --> 00:14:05.590
you know, it's kind of fart-ish.

00:14:05.590 --> 00:14:07.800
And it feels good.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:09.740
So again, kind of fart-ish.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:11.950
Mythical.com.

00:14:11.950 --> 00:14:14.670
That didn't, the words mythical--

00:14:14.670 --> 00:14:16.040
Mythical.com.

00:14:16.040 --> 00:14:17.990
Didn't come out of my mouth exactly right.

00:14:17.990 --> 00:14:20.863
There's also a, we don't have
our hands on it right now,

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.300
there's a Feel Good
Mythical Morning pop socket.

00:14:24.300 --> 00:14:26.550
So every time you look at
your phone and hold on to it,

00:14:26.550 --> 00:14:27.980
it feels good, and you know what?

00:14:27.980 --> 00:14:30.140
It feels good to pop in.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:32.670
I did get that, actually, I just remember,

00:14:32.670 --> 00:14:34.990
I got one of those in the mail I believe,

00:14:34.990 --> 00:14:37.570
and it's just in a package.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:38.920
Sorry, crew.

00:14:38.920 --> 00:14:40.930
Sorry Mythical crew who
sent that to me on time,

00:14:40.930 --> 00:14:41.960
I don't have it.

00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:43.520
But it feels good and it looks good.

00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:44.353
Go to mythical.com,

00:14:44.353 --> 00:14:46.880
we got all types of stuff
including pop sockets.

00:14:46.880 --> 00:14:50.730
We will just, let's get poppin'.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:53.537
If you want to know what's
poppin', go to mythical.com.

00:14:54.740 --> 00:14:56.400
Okay, back to the list.

00:14:56.400 --> 00:14:58.430
We're to your number nine.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.110
If you've lost track already.

00:15:00.110 --> 00:15:00.943
Number nine.

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:06.743
What I consider to be the perfect movie.

00:15:07.620 --> 00:15:09.733
Perfect movie and it's only number nine?

00:15:11.037 --> 00:15:12.800
"The Godfather."

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:14.610
Okay, okay.

00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:18.400
I thought that one of
these would make your list.

00:15:18.400 --> 00:15:21.083
I mean people say "Godfather
Part II" is better.

00:15:22.468 --> 00:15:26.270
Well, again, there's another trilogy,

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:27.860
as you might imagine, on my list,

00:15:27.860 --> 00:15:30.433
there's probably at
least one on your list.

00:15:32.240 --> 00:15:33.880
And I just pick the first one from,

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.940
I'm just picking the first
movie from the trilogy,

00:15:36.940 --> 00:15:38.447
and "Godfather II" is incredible.

00:15:38.447 --> 00:15:41.080
"Godfather III" is, everybody
sort of agrees is not as good,

00:15:41.080 --> 00:15:44.590
but I love a gangster movie.

00:15:44.590 --> 00:15:47.880
I absolutely, so obviously "The Godfather"

00:15:47.880 --> 00:15:49.000
was Francis Ford Coppola,

00:15:49.000 --> 00:15:51.130
and then Scorsese kind of came in

00:15:51.130 --> 00:15:52.890
and has taken over the mantle of

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:55.047
the making the perfect gangster movie.

00:15:55.047 --> 00:15:58.660
"Goodfellas," speaking of Scorsese, was,

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:00.260
almost took the spot on the list

00:16:00.260 --> 00:16:05.260
because "Goodfellas" is
funnier than "The Godfather."

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.350
And I also think it's a
perfect movie, by the way.

00:16:09.718 --> 00:16:12.860
And it's sort of the
modern gangster movie,

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:14.380
but a lot of the same characters,

00:16:14.380 --> 00:16:16.443
it's just you have the
addition of Joe Pesci.

00:16:17.420 --> 00:16:18.973
What they're playing the same characters?

00:16:18.973 --> 00:16:19.840
They're not playing the same characters.

00:16:19.840 --> 00:16:21.293
No, no, no, no, no, but I'm just saying.

00:16:21.293 --> 00:16:23.360
It's the same actors.

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:24.620
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:28.930
De Niro, you know,

00:16:28.930 --> 00:16:29.763
so,

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:34.770
I love the epic sort of like sweeping,

00:16:34.770 --> 00:16:38.880
multi-generational nature of
a really good gangster movie.

00:16:38.880 --> 00:16:40.270
And again, that's what
they did in the Irishman

00:16:40.270 --> 00:16:41.930
where they kind of took
him through his life

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:46.930
and I just sit there and I
just kind of just completely,

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.320
I become engrossed with this,

00:16:48.320 --> 00:16:49.910
and I remember the first time
I watched "The Godfather,"

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:51.860
which I don't even remember how old I was,

00:16:51.860 --> 00:16:54.263
but I was a teenager, and,

00:16:55.290 --> 00:16:58.360
I very rarely have
gotten lost in something

00:16:58.360 --> 00:17:00.062
and just been like,

00:17:00.062 --> 00:17:00.895
because a lot of people are like,

00:17:00.895 --> 00:17:02.430
man there's movies are so long,

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:04.880
like a Scorsese movie,
and "The Godfather,"

00:17:04.880 --> 00:17:07.430
I don't know how long the
first one is, they're all long,

00:17:07.430 --> 00:17:10.110
but like, it doesn't matter.

00:17:10.110 --> 00:17:11.760
I could just take it, I could watch it,

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.340
I could watch like a
12 hour version of it,

00:17:14.340 --> 00:17:17.090
because everything about it is perfect.

00:17:17.090 --> 00:17:20.470
The writing, the acting, the
score, the cinematography.

00:17:20.470 --> 00:17:23.360
I just don't think
there's any misses at all.

00:17:23.360 --> 00:17:27.343
Although I was surprised to
learn that Marlon Brando,

00:17:28.260 --> 00:17:29.093
two things about him,

00:17:29.093 --> 00:17:31.560
he actually put cotton balls in his cheeks

00:17:31.560 --> 00:17:34.060
when he did his audition for the role

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:38.030
because he wanted his face
to kind of like a bulldog,

00:17:38.030 --> 00:17:39.640
more so than an already did.

00:17:39.640 --> 00:17:42.730
And then for the movie they had a dentist

00:17:42.730 --> 00:17:45.220
make like a prosthetic
that goes in his mouth

00:17:45.220 --> 00:17:46.740
that pushes his cheeks out

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.610
and gives him that sort
of that way he talked.

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:50.443
Oh.

00:17:50.443 --> 00:17:52.690
But he also, he's such a weird dude,

00:17:52.690 --> 00:17:56.020
he didn't memorize any of
his lines for the movie,

00:17:56.020 --> 00:17:59.000
he read them off cue cards.

00:17:59.000 --> 00:18:00.160
Seriously?

00:18:00.160 --> 00:18:02.540
And so, to learn that the lead actor in

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:04.730
one of the best movies of all time

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:09.420
wasn't really even acting
in the traditional sense.

00:18:09.420 --> 00:18:10.253
Wow.

00:18:10.253 --> 00:18:12.920
Was a little surprising, but.

00:18:12.920 --> 00:18:14.150
So you're saying that a gangster,

00:18:14.150 --> 00:18:15.617
well, okay, you don't have to,

00:18:15.617 --> 00:18:17.740
but a gangster movie
didn't make your top 10?

00:18:17.740 --> 00:18:18.660
No gangster movie.

00:18:18.660 --> 00:18:20.560
And I, you know, I think a lot of people

00:18:20.560 --> 00:18:24.436
may be thinking, Link,
you've got a reputation

00:18:24.436 --> 00:18:29.000
of being the guy who
hasn't seen the movies.

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:29.880
And you know what, that's true.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:34.880
Especially, like all of the
movies that everybody's seen

00:18:34.900 --> 00:18:37.630
from the 80s, you know,
which is why we made that

00:18:37.630 --> 00:18:40.910
ViewMaster thing for the Mythical Society

00:18:40.910 --> 00:18:42.910
that put me in these
movies I've never seen

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:45.027
like "Ferris Bueller's Day Off."

00:18:46.380 --> 00:18:47.750
But yeah, I've seen "The Godfather."

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:48.790
I've seen "The Godfather Part II,"

00:18:48.790 --> 00:18:51.770
but there was a it was a stint in college

00:18:51.770 --> 00:18:55.940
where I realized I needed to
go back and watch these movies

00:18:55.940 --> 00:18:59.690
because in my house growing
up we just didn't watch,

00:18:59.690 --> 00:19:01.810
we didn't rent that many movies,
or watch that many movies.

00:19:01.810 --> 00:19:03.890
In your house, I just think that was like,

00:19:03.890 --> 00:19:05.983
that was a big thing, right?

00:19:06.989 --> 00:19:08.509
Yeah we watched a lot of movies.

00:19:08.509 --> 00:19:09.370
And not only did you watch them together,

00:19:09.370 --> 00:19:11.640
but you would also watch
the movies on your own.

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:16.130
But you'd rent those VHS of
"The Godfather," and watch that.

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:19.010
So in college, I watched those.

00:19:19.010 --> 00:19:22.590
I watched, you know, "Space Odyssey 2001,"

00:19:22.590 --> 00:19:25.440
you know I watched all
the things that, like--

00:19:25.440 --> 00:19:28.030
Hold on, hold on, so 2001
didn't make your top 10?

00:19:28.030 --> 00:19:28.863
No.

00:19:28.863 --> 00:19:29.760
That was an honorable mention for me.

00:19:29.760 --> 00:19:32.820
I was sure that that movie
would make your top 10.

00:19:32.820 --> 00:19:34.940
I had that I had that kick where I took

00:19:34.940 --> 00:19:39.060
an intro to film class my sophomore year,

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.380
and I really felt convicted to go back

00:19:42.380 --> 00:19:45.080
and watch the greatest movies of all time.

00:19:45.080 --> 00:19:47.610
And we would watch a lot of them together.

00:19:47.610 --> 00:19:49.700
And there was a really cool, you know,

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:52.640
video store there were a lot
of you could rent for $1,

00:19:52.640 --> 00:19:54.623
remember that?
Yeah.

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:58.930
So, I watched them all so closely together

00:19:58.930 --> 00:20:02.070
that they didn't have a
lasting personal impact.

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:05.050
There are some from that time in my life,

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:06.483
but it says a lot more about me

00:20:06.483 --> 00:20:08.123
than it does about the movie.

00:20:09.370 --> 00:20:14.003
So no, no gangster movies in my list.

00:20:15.670 --> 00:20:17.250
Okay, what is your number nine?

00:20:17.250 --> 00:20:20.350
My number nine is

00:20:21.310 --> 00:20:22.743
from 2014,

00:20:24.310 --> 00:20:27.060
I was making my list and I realized

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:30.720
I didn't have a Marvel movie on my list.

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:34.890
And I feel like if I'm
going to see a movie now,

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.340
like without exception,
I go to the theater

00:20:37.340 --> 00:20:40.760
to see a Marvel movie because
my entire family's into it,

00:20:40.760 --> 00:20:42.090
it's a way that we connect.

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:45.120
We had a lot of really great memories

00:20:45.120 --> 00:20:48.550
around watching movies,
we watched "Infinity War,"

00:20:48.550 --> 00:20:52.460
and when it was over, we
had reservations for dinner

00:20:52.460 --> 00:20:55.610
and Lily was in tears, and I was like,

00:20:55.610 --> 00:20:57.850
emotionally ripped apart.

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:00.800
And we didn't even go, we
didn't even eat dinner.

00:21:00.800 --> 00:21:03.353
We just went home, you know?

00:21:03.353 --> 00:21:07.860
So I felt like I had,
Marvel had to be represented

00:21:07.860 --> 00:21:09.530
on my list somewhere.

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:14.530
So at number nine, the only
Marvel movie on my list,

00:21:14.817 --> 00:21:17.210
"Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:21:17.210 --> 00:21:19.540
That's my favorite Marvel movie.

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:21.950
I mean, "Infinity War,"
the first "Avengers,"

00:21:21.950 --> 00:21:24.783
like, there's a lot of
ones that are special.

00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:29.490
But "Guardians" is just my favorite.

00:21:29.490 --> 00:21:31.830
It's just, it's the funniest.

00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:35.760
It was so surprising because I wasn't,

00:21:35.760 --> 00:21:38.470
I'm not a comic book guy so I wasn't

00:21:38.470 --> 00:21:40.930
really familiar with the characters.

00:21:40.930 --> 00:21:42.990
So, my expectations were low

00:21:42.990 --> 00:21:44.990
but then they were blown out of the water.

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:48.860
And it just continues to
deliver with every sequel.

00:21:48.860 --> 00:21:50.420
I can't wait for the next one.

00:21:50.420 --> 00:21:51.520
Yeah.

00:21:51.520 --> 00:21:55.690
So, "Guardians of the
Galaxy" was on my original,

00:21:55.690 --> 00:21:57.087
just like my long list.

00:21:57.087 --> 00:21:59.870
And I kind of, so I guess
you could technically say

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:02.890
it's on my honorable mentions,

00:22:02.890 --> 00:22:04.360
but it didn't really have it,

00:22:04.360 --> 00:22:06.270
I knew it wasn't gonna
make it into the top 10.

00:22:06.270 --> 00:22:07.103
Yeah.

00:22:07.103 --> 00:22:09.780
But it is, for me, as a guy who

00:22:09.780 --> 00:22:11.680
doesn't really like comic book movies,

00:22:12.520 --> 00:22:17.480
it is easily the best franchise,
Marvel franchise for me.

00:22:17.480 --> 00:22:19.020
And I think that's why it's on my list

00:22:19.020 --> 00:22:21.270
for the reason it's almost on your list.

00:22:21.270 --> 00:22:23.693
Plus all the reasons I love Marvel.

00:22:24.675 --> 00:22:26.120
And one of the things that happens in

00:22:26.120 --> 00:22:29.023
Marvel movies that is
frustrating to me is just,

00:22:30.684 --> 00:22:32.850
I'm just not, the whole
action thing is not,

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:35.820
it's not super appealing to me, and like,

00:22:35.820 --> 00:22:38.003
following a bunch of different,

00:22:39.190 --> 00:22:41.740
like I like "The Avengers" and
they're incredibly well done,

00:22:41.740 --> 00:22:43.850
but, like, just things
get too complicated.

00:22:43.850 --> 00:22:45.150
It's not like it's difficult to follow

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:46.170
what's happening in "The Avengers,"

00:22:46.170 --> 00:22:48.610
I'm not saying that, it's not
like "Memento," or something,

00:22:48.610 --> 00:22:51.450
but it's--
There's emotional investment

00:22:51.450 --> 00:22:53.700
in the entire universe.

00:22:53.700 --> 00:22:56.610
It gets a little too complicated
and too complex for me

00:22:56.610 --> 00:22:59.773
to like find out what I'm
supposed to attach to,

00:22:59.773 --> 00:23:01.500
but for "Guardians of the Galaxy,"

00:23:01.500 --> 00:23:04.350
I attach, I find that there's
an emotional connection,

00:23:04.350 --> 00:23:06.979
the soundtrack, we play the soundtrack,

00:23:06.979 --> 00:23:10.610
by far the most played movie soundtrack

00:23:10.610 --> 00:23:12.180
in our house is "Guardians of the Galaxy."

00:23:12.180 --> 00:23:14.273
It's going all the time.

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.240
Because, you know, what Chris Pratt

00:23:17.240 --> 00:23:19.920
listens to in the movie, it's incredible.

00:23:19.920 --> 00:23:21.210
And it's super funny.

00:23:21.210 --> 00:23:22.810
So yeah, I definitely thought about it,

00:23:22.810 --> 00:23:26.340
but I just couldn't give it a top 10 spot.

00:23:26.340 --> 00:23:28.623
Alright, so, where you at?

00:23:29.660 --> 00:23:30.610
Number eight.

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:34.410
I'm almost positive this
is not on your list.

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:37.150
Unless you're, you got something that

00:23:37.150 --> 00:23:38.200
you've never told me.

00:23:39.447 --> 00:23:40.830
"The Wizard of Oz."

00:23:40.830 --> 00:23:42.300
Wow.

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:43.554
Really?

00:23:43.554 --> 00:23:44.387
Okay.

00:23:44.387 --> 00:23:45.250
Yeah, oh yeah.

