My dad took me to see

00:35:49.527 --> 00:35:52.647
"Indiana Jones and the Temple of Doom."

00:35:55.150 --> 00:36:00.150
And in a recent episode,
well maybe months back,

00:36:00.552 --> 00:36:03.500
I did talk about how we watched
all of the "Indiana Jones"

00:36:03.500 --> 00:36:05.700
movies with the kids, so
I introduced them to them,

00:36:05.700 --> 00:36:08.540
and they were enthralled as well.

00:36:08.540 --> 00:36:11.180
Now, you could make the argument

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:16.143
that "Raiders of the Lost
Ark" is a better movie.

00:36:17.970 --> 00:36:19.100
I would make that argument.

00:36:19.100 --> 00:36:22.240
And "Raiders of the Lost Ark"
is on my honorable mentions.

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:25.779
But they both hold up, big time.

00:36:25.779 --> 00:36:26.612
Oh they're great.

00:36:26.612 --> 00:36:28.147
"Temple of Doom" is a lot darker.

00:36:28.147 --> 00:36:31.293
"Temple of Doom" is not as funny,

00:36:32.520 --> 00:36:35.020
but it holds a special place because

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:38.370
I don't know why my dad
took me to see this movie.

00:36:38.370 --> 00:36:40.410
It was rated PG.

00:36:40.410 --> 00:36:45.410
And then, because of the public reaction

00:36:46.190 --> 00:36:50.820
to the darkness of "Gremlins,"
and "Temple of Doom,"

00:36:50.820 --> 00:36:54.520
Spielberg lobbied that they
come up with a new rating,

00:36:54.520 --> 00:36:57.700
which became PG-13, and
they change the rating

00:36:57.700 --> 00:36:59.720
of "Temple of Doom" to PG-13.

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:02.320
One of the first movies
that got that rating.

00:37:02.320 --> 00:37:04.520
So I just remember seeing the heart come

00:37:04.520 --> 00:37:07.040
out of that guy's chest
and covering my eyes

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:10.130
in the theater and being
absolutely horrified.

00:37:10.130 --> 00:37:11.700
And it was amazing.

00:37:11.700 --> 00:37:12.940
Watching the movie back,

00:37:12.940 --> 00:37:17.880
I couldn't believe how
endlessly entertaining it was.

00:37:17.880 --> 00:37:21.310
One scene to the next you're just thrown

00:37:21.310 --> 00:37:25.030
as he tumbles from one thing to the next

00:37:25.030 --> 00:37:29.790
and it's absolutely endless, it seems.

00:37:29.790 --> 00:37:33.050
Like you, you think it
can't get better than this,

00:37:33.050 --> 00:37:35.520
the action can't get
more exciting than this,

00:37:35.520 --> 00:37:36.550
and then it happens.

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:40.110
And it's, I mean, there are some problems,

00:37:40.110 --> 00:37:44.707
you know, the cultural
portrayals of, you know,

00:37:46.300 --> 00:37:48.340
and then like there's
the white savior thing,

00:37:48.340 --> 00:37:50.680
I'm not saying it doesn't
have its problems,

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:53.340
and I'm not, I'm not defending those.

00:37:53.340 --> 00:37:55.310
But, it holds a special place in my heart

00:37:55.310 --> 00:37:59.530
because it scared the
crap out of me as a child,

00:37:59.530 --> 00:38:02.940
and my kids loved it when they watched it.

00:38:02.940 --> 00:38:05.393
It scared the crap out of
Lando, and he loved it,

00:38:06.430 --> 00:38:07.770
a few weeks ago, so.

00:38:07.770 --> 00:38:11.287
I definitely feel like, like I said,

00:38:11.287 --> 00:38:12.310
"Raiders of the Lost Ark"

00:38:12.310 --> 00:38:14.900
was was very close to making my top 10.

00:38:14.900 --> 00:38:16.980
I kind of felt like I had to,

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:19.350
I wanted to put a Spielberg
movie in my top 10,

00:38:19.350 --> 00:38:22.120
because that's how I ended up
kind of approaching this list

00:38:22.120 --> 00:38:23.550
in a lot of ways, it was like,

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.670
I started realizing
filmmakers that I liked

00:38:26.670 --> 00:38:28.160
their body of work the most,

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:30.160
and I was like, okay, I'm
gonna give you one film,

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:33.070
but I just, I couldn't do it

00:38:33.070 --> 00:38:36.470
as much as I absolutely love
the Indiana Jones franchise,

00:38:36.470 --> 00:38:39.210
and it does loom very
large in my childhood.

00:38:39.210 --> 00:38:40.930
And you got like the dinner scene

00:38:40.930 --> 00:38:44.770
where they're like eating the
bugs and the monkey brains.

00:38:44.770 --> 00:38:45.746
There's so many iconic scenes.

00:38:45.746 --> 00:38:48.980
That's GMM right there, man.

00:38:48.980 --> 00:38:52.770
It's like, the thing that I
was horrified by as a child

00:38:52.770 --> 00:38:57.770
is something that became a
cornerstone of my career path.

00:38:57.840 --> 00:39:00.130
I've got a connection to this movie man.

00:39:00.130 --> 00:39:03.030
Okay, my number six

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:08.840
is by far my most recent movie.

00:39:08.840 --> 00:39:09.880
Okay.

00:39:09.880 --> 00:39:11.490
I don't have my years in here,

00:39:11.490 --> 00:39:12.323
I should have done that,

00:39:12.323 --> 00:39:15.010
but this is just from a couple
years ago, few years ago.

00:39:16.897 --> 00:39:18.450
"Get Out."

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:20.320
Yeah, I assumed, "Get Out"

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:22.910
was a was an honorable mention of mine.

00:39:22.910 --> 00:39:24.660
Like I knew you had to hit horror,

00:39:24.660 --> 00:39:26.373
and I knew even independent from that

00:39:26.373 --> 00:39:28.453
you had to hit "Get Out," because,

00:39:29.370 --> 00:39:31.810
it's so pivotal so culturally.

00:39:31.810 --> 00:39:35.870
You might think, yeah, you would assume

00:39:35.870 --> 00:39:37.560
there'd be more horror on my,

00:39:37.560 --> 00:39:39.743
I like horror movies a lot, but,

00:39:41.010 --> 00:39:43.180
and I enjoy watching them,

00:39:43.180 --> 00:39:44.300
probably more than any movie

00:39:44.300 --> 00:39:46.400
because I just enjoy the
idea of getting scared,

00:39:46.400 --> 00:39:47.680
especially in a group of people.

00:39:47.680 --> 00:39:50.440
And now Locke and I, every
single Saturday night

00:39:50.440 --> 00:39:51.780
is horror movie night and the two of us

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:56.150
just go down into the garage
where we've got a television,

00:39:56.150 --> 00:39:57.520
and no one else will get scared

00:39:57.520 --> 00:39:59.680
because no one else can
hear what's happening.

00:39:59.680 --> 00:40:02.920
But we've watched a bunch
of ridiculous horror movies.

00:40:02.920 --> 00:40:03.753
But anyway.

00:40:03.753 --> 00:40:05.230
Did you think about "Us,"

00:40:05.230 --> 00:40:07.197
or is it clearly "Get Out" over "Us?"

00:40:07.197 --> 00:40:08.710
"Get Out" is better than "Us," yeah.

00:40:08.710 --> 00:40:11.120
I mean, there's so many things,

00:40:11.120 --> 00:40:13.493
I love everything about both movies,

00:40:14.590 --> 00:40:19.590
but "Get Out" was one
of the most enjoyable

00:40:20.690 --> 00:40:22.940
movie going experiences I've ever had,

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:25.140
like the first time I
saw it in the theater.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:30.200
I was feeling so many things all at once

00:40:30.200 --> 00:40:31.210
when I was watching it.

00:40:31.210 --> 00:40:35.330
The first thing I was feeling
was this is super scary,

00:40:35.330 --> 00:40:37.090
and it's like getting me,

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:41.501
but this is super funny,
and like a really funny,

00:40:41.501 --> 00:40:45.250
like a I'm laughing as much

00:40:45.250 --> 00:40:49.080
as I am kind of cringing in fear.

00:40:49.080 --> 00:40:51.650
And then the whole time I'm thinking,

00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:53.280
Jordan Peele make this, you know,

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:58.060
it's just like Jordan Peele
of Key and Peele, comedy duo,

00:40:58.060 --> 00:41:02.800
this guy has gone and
created this perfect movie,

00:41:02.800 --> 00:41:04.460
there was this, there
was like a combination,

00:41:04.460 --> 00:41:06.370
there was a fascination,

00:41:06.370 --> 00:41:10.570
but also like an incredible
jealousy at the same time

00:41:10.570 --> 00:41:12.980
to just think, dude,
this, this guy went off

00:41:12.980 --> 00:41:14.420
and did this thing that, man,

00:41:14.420 --> 00:41:16.900
I mean like, this is an
incredible accomplishment

00:41:16.900 --> 00:41:19.450
for anyone, but this dude
that was just over here

00:41:19.450 --> 00:41:22.760
doing sketch comedy had this
up his sleeve the whole time?

00:41:22.760 --> 00:41:23.783
This is crazy.

00:41:25.580 --> 00:41:28.210
But yeah, it's, I mean it's
one of my favorite movies.

00:41:28.210 --> 00:41:30.383
Obviously it's number six on my list, but,

00:41:32.300 --> 00:41:36.630
it just, I think it also sort
of represented the cultural

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:42.373
resurrection of horror,

00:41:44.040 --> 00:41:47.020
bringing horror back into the mainstream

00:41:47.020 --> 00:41:48.690
in a really big way.

00:41:48.690 --> 00:41:52.120
A lot of people have seen
a lot more horror movies,

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:54.770
and horror movies are
grossing a lot more money

00:41:54.770 --> 00:41:57.840
at the box office because of "Get Out"

00:41:57.840 --> 00:42:01.290
and what it kind of did
for people getting like,

00:42:01.290 --> 00:42:04.010
oh, I get it, it's kind of
like riding a roller coaster.

00:42:04.010 --> 00:42:06.210
As opposed to just being
a bunch of horror scenes

00:42:06.210 --> 00:42:08.460
going in and watching
people get slaughtered

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:09.410
in a movie theater.

00:42:11.780 --> 00:42:16.483
My number six is my favorite, well,

00:42:17.490 --> 00:42:20.080
I don't know, if you ask me
what my favorite comedy is,

00:42:20.080 --> 00:42:23.570
it's weird because I have
comedies higher than this one,

00:42:23.570 --> 00:42:27.120
but, I think of this as more of my like

00:42:27.120 --> 00:42:28.749
if I'm going to go with like my,

00:42:28.749 --> 00:42:31.080
the strictest version of comedy

00:42:31.080 --> 00:42:33.800
that I'm going to apply to
something then make that list,

00:42:33.800 --> 00:42:35.453
I think this is my number one.

00:42:37.282 --> 00:42:39.730
My favorite just straight up comedy.

00:42:39.730 --> 00:42:43.857
Released in 1998, "The Big Lebowski."

00:42:45.590 --> 00:42:46.549
Wow.

00:42:46.549 --> 00:42:47.382
You thought this would be higher.

00:42:47.382 --> 00:42:48.570
Is your number six.

00:42:48.570 --> 00:42:49.873
That's my number six.

00:42:50.930 --> 00:42:51.763
Yep.

00:42:51.763 --> 00:42:52.923
You thought it would be higher.

00:42:52.923 --> 00:42:55.540
Well it's higher on my list
than it's on your list.

00:42:55.540 --> 00:42:57.120
Okay.

00:42:57.120 --> 00:43:00.343
But so you thought that I
liked the movie more than you.

00:43:01.540 --> 00:43:02.560
I thought that it would,

00:43:02.560 --> 00:43:06.270
I thought that it might be
your number one, honestly.

00:43:06.270 --> 00:43:08.530
Yeah, at certain points in my life,

00:43:08.530 --> 00:43:13.270
it would be, but, there's
other movies that just,

00:43:13.270 --> 00:43:15.250
they resonate, they have more meaning,

00:43:15.250 --> 00:43:17.460
they touch my life in a lot more areas.

00:43:17.460 --> 00:43:19.920
This just kind of touches my funny bone

00:43:19.920 --> 00:43:22.053
and my comedic sensibility.

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:27.500
I really regret not seeing
"The Big Lebowski" in theaters.

00:43:27.500 --> 00:43:30.343
I think that was my introduction
to the Coen brothers.

00:43:32.410 --> 00:43:34.340
This was, again, I was taking this

00:43:34.340 --> 00:43:37.943
intro to film class
sophomore year in college,

00:43:39.939 --> 00:43:41.840
I was able to rent it

00:43:41.840 --> 00:43:44.860
because they were talking
about it in class.

00:43:44.860 --> 00:43:49.020
And I was like, how have I
not heard about this movie?

00:43:49.020 --> 00:43:52.050
It's absolutely hilarious.

00:43:52.050 --> 00:43:55.633
It's also very profane,
and that's really edgy.

00:43:57.220 --> 00:43:59.350
It was, I wrote, I had to write

00:43:59.350 --> 00:44:02.860
a paper about mise-en-scènee.

00:44:02.860 --> 00:44:07.320
Which is basically
arranging scenery and props.

00:44:07.320 --> 00:44:08.870
And so I wrote a whole paper.

00:44:08.870 --> 00:44:11.210
I'm horrible at writing papers.

00:44:11.210 --> 00:44:14.730
But I had to watch the movie,
again and again and again

00:44:14.730 --> 00:44:17.170
in order to piece together this paper.

00:44:17.170 --> 00:44:20.250
So I got really acquainted
with it, but it never got old.

00:44:20.250 --> 00:44:24.970
The performances from Jeff Bridges,

00:44:24.970 --> 00:44:29.970
and John Goodman are just,
I feel like they're perfect.

00:44:30.300 --> 00:44:33.330
And then the ways that they
interact and, you know,

00:44:33.330 --> 00:44:35.430
it was the first thing that
I really started to notice,

00:44:35.430 --> 00:44:36.880
because I don't watch a whole,

00:44:36.880 --> 00:44:39.810
I don't watch a lot of movies
again and again and again.

00:44:39.810 --> 00:44:42.440
That's just not, that's just not me.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:45.790
But in such a short
span of time to do that

00:44:45.790 --> 00:44:48.260
and still enjoy it,
and start to appreciate

00:44:48.260 --> 00:44:53.260
the nuances of acting, I
think for the first time,

00:44:53.750 --> 00:44:55.770
was another touch point for this movie.

00:44:55.770 --> 00:45:00.040
But it was just, it was just so funny,

00:45:00.040 --> 00:45:02.630
and still is so funny.

00:45:02.630 --> 00:45:05.170
Yeah, I gotta say, it's my number two.

00:45:05.170 --> 00:45:06.173
Wow!

00:45:07.920 --> 00:45:08.753
Yeah.

00:45:08.753 --> 00:45:10.120
When's the last time you watched it?

00:45:13.100 --> 00:45:14.410
It's been years.

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:17.410
It's been years, and
I was talking to Locke

00:45:17.410 --> 00:45:19.103
about how we should watch it together.

00:45:20.520 --> 00:45:22.473
But I do, I know that one of the things

00:45:22.473 --> 00:45:25.820
that people discover when they watch it,

00:45:25.820 --> 00:45:27.170
and again, Coen Brothers

00:45:27.170 --> 00:45:28.670
are some of my favorite
filmmakers of all time.

00:45:28.670 --> 00:45:32.060
I wanted them to have a spot,

00:45:32.060 --> 00:45:34.260
and this is my favorite
Coen Brothers movie.

00:45:35.970 --> 00:45:38.894
The reason that it, well, lot of people

00:45:38.894 --> 00:45:40.940
talk about the fact that nothing happens,

00:45:40.940 --> 00:45:42.920
and I was actually reading
an interview with Joel Coen,

00:45:42.920 --> 00:45:45.497
who says, "You know the
plot is sort of secondary

00:45:45.497 --> 00:45:48.197
"to the other things that are
sort of going on in the piece.

00:45:48.197 --> 00:45:49.987
"I think that if people
get a little confused,

00:45:49.987 --> 00:45:51.307
"it's not necessarily
going to get in the way

00:45:51.307 --> 00:45:52.930
"of them enjoying the movie."

00:45:52.930 --> 00:45:55.190
So, in this interview
goes on to talk about

00:45:55.190 --> 00:45:59.393
how like the plot is like
the fourth or fifth thing,

00:45:59.393 --> 00:46:02.760
because there's an
aesthetic quality to it,

00:46:02.760 --> 00:46:06.683
there's the characters, the
cut, the dialogue is incredible.

00:46:06.683 --> 00:46:08.337
The situations.

00:46:08.337 --> 00:46:11.490
I mean, like the baseball bat to the car,

00:46:11.490 --> 00:46:15.170
with the homework under
the seat, you know it's.

00:46:15.170 --> 00:46:16.803
Well and I think that it,

00:46:17.950 --> 00:46:20.380
yeah I struggle between, like,

00:46:20.380 --> 00:46:24.510
my list is a combination of
movies that do something to me

00:46:24.510 --> 00:46:29.460
personally as a, just a movie watcher,

00:46:29.460 --> 00:46:31.410
you know, as an audience member.

00:46:31.410 --> 00:46:33.190
And then there are
movies that do something

00:46:33.190 --> 00:46:37.150
for me as an audience
member, and aspiring artists.

00:46:37.150 --> 00:46:39.143
And so, again, when I watch something

00:46:39.143 --> 00:46:41.230
that Coen Brothers make,

00:46:41.230 --> 00:46:43.000
I think a lot of it
has to do with the fact

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:44.250
that they're a duo, you know,

00:46:44.250 --> 00:46:46.410
a duo that's known each other
forever as being brothers,

00:46:46.410 --> 00:46:48.500
and so I think that when we look at them

00:46:48.500 --> 00:46:52.110
we think a lot about our
careers and, you know,

00:46:52.110 --> 00:46:53.240
we both read the book about

00:46:53.240 --> 00:46:55.453
their sort of creative process, years ago.

00:46:57.655 --> 00:46:59.090
And there's just something,

00:46:59.090 --> 00:47:01.190
there's something
inspirational and aspirational

00:47:01.190 --> 00:47:03.350
about them and their career,

00:47:03.350 --> 00:47:05.610
and I feel like this is like the pinnacle

00:47:05.610 --> 00:47:09.028
of where everything came
together in one movie for them.

00:47:09.028 --> 00:47:12.430
And so, again, it's a kind of thing that,

00:47:12.430 --> 00:47:14.060
and we always referenced this,

00:47:14.060 --> 00:47:16.950
we talk, when we're developing
characters for ourselves

00:47:16.950 --> 00:47:19.350
in some of the narrative
stuff that we've done,

00:47:19.350 --> 00:47:21.163
we think about the dynamic,

00:47:22.240 --> 00:47:27.060
we use those three characters
as points of reference

00:47:28.803 --> 00:47:30.420
in a lot of the stuff that we create.

00:47:30.420 --> 00:47:32.538
Like are you getting too Goodman here?

00:47:32.538 --> 00:47:36.059
How Bridges are you,
or how Buscemi are you,

00:47:36.059 --> 00:47:38.080
is your character, you know?

00:47:38.080 --> 00:47:39.470
I think that there's just something

00:47:39.470 --> 00:47:42.740
about that trio characters,
the dynamic's incredible.

00:47:42.740 --> 00:47:43.700
Yeah, my number two,

00:47:43.700 --> 00:47:45.503
and I thought it was going
to be your number one.

00:47:45.503 --> 00:47:47.793
Your number six, wow, okay, I'm very,

00:47:48.640 --> 00:47:49.720
I'm very interested to see

00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:51.410
where your list goes from this, from here.

00:47:51.410 --> 00:47:53.451
Okay, okay.

00:47:53.451 --> 00:47:58.451
My number five is, again, I
think it's a perfect movie,

00:48:02.447 --> 00:48:03.907
"Back to the Future."

00:48:03.907 --> 00:48:05.627
"Back to the Future."

00:48:06.540 --> 00:48:09.683
Love everything about this movie.

00:48:11.600 --> 00:48:13.853
A lot of people say
it's the perfect movie.

00:48:14.990 --> 00:48:19.110
You know, it is not the one
where he goes to the future.

00:48:19.110 --> 00:48:19.943
That's a bummer.

00:48:19.943 --> 00:48:21.200
No.

00:48:21.200 --> 00:48:24.670
Well, a lot of, it's interesting
because when I watched it,

00:48:24.670 --> 00:48:26.220
I thought to myself, I was like man,

00:48:26.220 --> 00:48:29.933
this movie has everything
that I want in a movie.

00:48:31.780 --> 00:48:33.740
You know, it's got this sort of

00:48:33.740 --> 00:48:35.420
reality bending nature
that if you're going

00:48:35.420 --> 00:48:37.870
to make a movie, you might as
well do something mythical.

00:48:37.870 --> 00:48:40.203
You know, I'll always say that,

00:48:41.700 --> 00:48:44.060
something magical should happen.

00:48:44.060 --> 00:48:46.913
So you got that sci-fi
element, it's incredibly funny.

00:48:48.835 --> 00:48:51.035
And of course all the
choices that he makes,

00:48:52.530 --> 00:48:55.280
are, I just think it's incredible

00:48:55.280 --> 00:48:56.610
the way that it progresses.

00:48:56.610 --> 00:48:58.170
But there's a lot of people who point out

00:48:58.170 --> 00:49:01.510
the fact that Marty McFly
really doesn't grow or change,

00:49:01.510 --> 00:49:02.920
which isn't really unusual thing,

00:49:02.920 --> 00:49:05.370
which would normally happen in a movie,

00:49:05.370 --> 00:49:07.820
is that it doesn't seem that
he really learns anything,

00:49:07.820 --> 00:49:11.450
there's no like epiphany for him really.

00:49:11.450 --> 00:49:12.283
But,

00:49:14.650 --> 00:49:16.250
yeah, I just,

00:49:16.250 --> 00:49:19.310
I watched this fairly recently,
like in the past five years,

00:49:19.310 --> 00:49:20.143
and it was when it hit me,

00:49:20.143 --> 00:49:23.690
I was like man I never, like
I loved this movie growing up,

00:49:23.690 --> 00:49:26.210
but I just kind of, I lumped it together

00:49:26.210 --> 00:49:29.940
with a lot of other
movies in that, you know,

00:49:29.940 --> 00:49:32.340
you got all the Spielberg stuff and then

00:49:33.330 --> 00:49:34.740
Zemeckis, of course, did this one.

00:49:34.740 --> 00:49:36.430
But it's a very Spielberg-y type,

00:49:36.430 --> 00:49:39.140
a lot of people just think
that Spielberg made it.

00:49:39.140 --> 00:49:41.160
I did think he was a producer on it.

00:49:41.160 --> 00:49:46.160
But Zemeckis, and then Robert
Gale wrote it together,

00:49:46.930 --> 00:49:50.360
which, this is a really interesting thing,

00:49:50.360 --> 00:49:51.430
I was reading about this,

00:49:51.430 --> 00:49:54.042
in a 2015 interview, Zemeckis maintained,

00:49:54.042 --> 00:49:58.560
so he and, Zemeckis
and Gale own the rights

00:49:58.560 --> 00:50:00.600
to distribution of the film.

00:50:00.600 --> 00:50:04.570
And the rights for all sequels.

00:50:04.570 --> 00:50:07.180
And they say that both of them have agreed

00:50:07.180 --> 00:50:11.203
that no reboots will be made
as long as they are alive.

00:50:12.120 --> 00:50:14.520
So don't expect, it's one of those things.

00:50:14.520 --> 00:50:16.860
and I love that because you
know they would screw it up.

00:50:16.860 --> 00:50:19.230
It's one of those things that they would,

00:50:19.230 --> 00:50:22.030
there's movie studios that
are chomping at the bit

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:25.370
to remake this movie knowing
that it would do really well

00:50:25.370 --> 00:50:26.203
at the box office,

00:50:26.203 --> 00:50:28.430
but you just can't
improve upon the original.

00:50:28.430 --> 00:50:29.554
This is just one of those cases,

00:50:29.554 --> 00:50:31.307
usually can't, but this
is just one of those cases

00:50:31.307 --> 00:50:33.590
that there is something sacred about it.

00:50:33.590 --> 00:50:34.423
Yeah.

00:50:34.423 --> 00:50:35.890
It's the ultimate time travel movie.

00:50:35.890 --> 00:50:39.860
The, and it's ultimate miss on my part.

00:50:39.860 --> 00:50:42.860
You know, I think that that movie,

00:50:42.860 --> 00:50:45.120
the fact that I didn't see that until

00:50:46.060 --> 00:50:48.330
college in its completion,

00:50:48.330 --> 00:50:50.140
maybe after college, again, is just,

00:50:50.140 --> 00:50:53.587
I know that's mind blowing,
and it's just, you know--

00:50:55.190 --> 00:50:56.503
You were deprived.

00:50:57.670 --> 00:51:00.220
A childhood cut short or
something, I don't know.

00:51:01.960 --> 00:51:04.353
Okay, so now we're at my number five.

00:51:05.830 --> 00:51:10.830
Okay, so this is where I've
placed the Star Wars trilogy,

00:51:11.720 --> 00:51:15.530
and I have made a choice of which one.

00:51:15.530 --> 00:51:18.190
But yeah, here we are at number five,

00:51:18.190 --> 00:51:20.100
with "The Empire Strikes Back."

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:23.230
I mean you can't argue, I
don't think you can argue

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:25.090
with that being the one
that you would choose

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:27.460
if you had to choose only one to watch

00:51:27.460 --> 00:51:29.433
or to rank is your number one of,

00:51:30.840 --> 00:51:33.473
you know, all nine of those.

00:51:35.410 --> 00:51:37.500
But I did actually, I'm
just gonna be honest,

00:51:37.500 --> 00:51:40.130
I did think a little bit
about "The Force Awakens,"

00:51:40.130 --> 00:51:43.550
because for me personally,
it was, you know,

00:51:43.550 --> 00:51:46.753
it was an opportunity to see
it, I don't remember seeing,

00:51:47.865 --> 00:51:49.210
I think I can almost,

00:51:49.210 --> 00:51:53.380
I don't remember seeing "Return
to the Jedi" in the theater.

00:51:53.380 --> 00:51:58.380
So, it was, "The Force Awakens,"

00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:01.260
that theater experience
after having waited so long

00:52:01.260 --> 00:52:03.330
and then it being a shared
experience with my kids

00:52:03.330 --> 00:52:07.493
and now Lily is, my whole
family, but most of all,

00:52:09.310 --> 00:52:11.970
Lily is so into Star Wars,

00:52:11.970 --> 00:52:14.720
to create that experience
and have that with her

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:15.700
is why "The Force Awakens,"

00:52:15.700 --> 00:52:17.693
and it's just such a nostalgic movie,

00:52:17.693 --> 00:52:20.930
because it's, you can
argue this is a remake

00:52:22.680 --> 00:52:25.670
of "A New Hope," but I love that one,

00:52:25.670 --> 00:52:27.470
but I'm still putting
"The Empire Strikes Back."

00:52:27.470 --> 00:52:32.470
I wish I could have experienced that,

00:52:33.220 --> 00:52:38.220
no, I am your father moment,
you know, for the first time,

00:52:38.860 --> 00:52:43.070
actually experienced it,
that would have been amazing,

00:52:43.070 --> 00:52:47.650
but, you know, everybody, by
the time we watched it we knew.

00:52:47.650 --> 00:52:50.560
I don't remember not
knowing before I watched it.

00:52:50.560 --> 00:52:51.609
Oh, oh yeah.

00:52:51.609 --> 00:52:53.428
Especially in the theater.

00:52:53.428 --> 00:52:55.603
I know I saw,

00:52:57.660 --> 00:53:00.810
I don't think I saw any of the
first in the theater because,

00:53:00.810 --> 00:53:03.673
I mean, that was '77, '80, and '83, right?

00:53:04.560 --> 00:53:06.067
I think so.

00:53:06.067 --> 00:53:08.010
So.
Right.

00:53:08.010 --> 00:53:10.400
Yeah, I ended up watching
all those on VHS.

00:53:10.400 --> 00:53:12.530
And I've never been, I love,

00:53:12.530 --> 00:53:17.530
I am a fan of Star Wars,
and I like the most recent,

00:53:20.910 --> 00:53:24.240
I really enjoy all the most recent ones.

00:53:24.240 --> 00:53:26.560
But, yeah, it didn't even get,

00:53:26.560 --> 00:53:28.830
they just didn't make an honorable mention

00:53:28.830 --> 00:53:30.783
because another trilogy did, which I have,

00:53:30.783 --> 00:53:34.210
I assume that another
trilogy also made your list,

00:53:34.210 --> 00:53:37.183
which I enjoy more, which
we've talked about, but.

00:53:37.183 --> 00:53:38.613
Listen, you don't have to apologize

00:53:38.613 --> 00:53:40.210
for not putting Star Wars on your list,

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:45.020
you just have to endure the
wrath of all the Star Wars fans.

00:53:45.020 --> 00:53:48.930
I think it's, the thing
I respect about Star Wars

00:53:48.930 --> 00:53:53.220
is what it, is the mythology and sort of

00:53:53.220 --> 00:53:56.400
the philosophy that goes into it

00:53:56.400 --> 00:53:58.130
and how it translates into so relatable,

00:53:58.130 --> 00:54:01.553
and you can use it in
so many illustrations.

00:54:02.874 --> 00:54:05.690
But the actual just sitting
there and watching the movie,

00:54:05.690 --> 00:54:08.950
especially the originals
it just doesn't get me,

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:12.350
it doesn't get me enough to
get into my favorite movies.

00:54:12.350 --> 00:54:14.060
But you know what does?

00:54:14.060 --> 00:54:16.410
Another movie that you're not gonna be

00:54:16.410 --> 00:54:17.340
surprised it's on my list,

00:54:17.340 --> 00:54:21.410
and I would bet anything
that is not on your list.

00:54:21.410 --> 00:54:24.500
Number four, "Braveheart."

00:54:24.500 --> 00:54:27.040
Oh, "Braveheart" was an
honorable mention for me.

00:54:27.040 --> 00:54:29.770
I remember we were both
really into that in college.

00:54:29.770 --> 00:54:31.920
I remember you'd be really
into the soundtrack,

00:54:31.920 --> 00:54:32.753
like you own the CD.

00:54:32.753 --> 00:54:36.580
Oh I had the soundtrack
and played it in my room.

00:54:36.580 --> 00:54:40.170
Now, I don't know if it was
something about the fact that

00:54:40.170 --> 00:54:44.320
I felt this like connection
with William Wallace,

00:54:44.320 --> 00:54:45.840
because you know, I always
thought I was Scottish

00:54:45.840 --> 00:54:47.450
even though I'm more Irish.

00:54:47.450 --> 00:54:52.450
But there's something, it's
one of those movies where

00:54:53.040 --> 00:54:57.850
I get so, again, nothing
about the movie appeals to me

00:54:57.850 --> 00:55:01.030
from the in the
aspirational filmmaker side.

00:55:01.030 --> 00:55:03.690
It's one of those movies
that I'm just completely

00:55:04.550 --> 00:55:09.340
an audience member, and I get
completely lost in the story.

00:55:09.340 --> 00:55:12.830
And every single thing about it, I just,

00:55:12.830 --> 00:55:15.780
you feel like your heart
is just like beating

00:55:15.780 --> 00:55:17.760
bigger and bigger throughout the movie,

00:55:17.760 --> 00:55:20.383
and then the,

00:55:21.560 --> 00:55:23.760
the ending of that movie.

00:55:23.760 --> 00:55:24.593
Oh gosh.

00:55:24.593 --> 00:55:25.860
The ending of that movie is just like,

00:55:25.860 --> 00:55:28.750
it's like the first time I remember like

00:55:28.750 --> 00:55:30.900
almost like weeping to the point of like

00:55:30.900 --> 00:55:34.970
shoulder convulsions in a movie theater.

00:55:34.970 --> 00:55:36.550
And it just something about the way

00:55:36.550 --> 00:55:40.320
it emotionally connected with
me at that time in my life,

00:55:40.320 --> 00:55:43.270
is, and I was talking to the
boys, we haven't watched it.

00:55:43.270 --> 00:55:44.570
Yeah, it's very intense.

00:55:45.850 --> 00:55:47.080
Yeah.

00:55:47.080 --> 00:55:50.420
And, you know, it's also very funny.

00:55:50.420 --> 00:55:51.400
There's funny moments

00:55:51.400 --> 00:55:54.283
and you really care
about those characters.

00:55:56.130 --> 00:55:59.133
And it's just sort of a,

00:56:00.580 --> 00:56:03.870
it's a huge milestone,
kind of accomplishment,

00:56:03.870 --> 00:56:07.023
in filmmaking, in just like an epic,

00:56:09.260 --> 00:56:11.900
the scale of it, and the intensity.

00:56:11.900 --> 00:56:15.170
I think it's one of the
first movies where there was,

00:56:15.170 --> 00:56:16.410
they just made the decision to be like,

00:56:16.410 --> 00:56:20.070
hey, when we show people
fighting like it's gonna be real,

00:56:20.070 --> 00:56:22.080
like there's gonna be like swords

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:24.820
going into people.
Thousands of people died

00:56:24.820 --> 00:56:27.269
because swords really went into people.

00:56:27.269 --> 00:56:30.593
Well, interestingly, one of
the things I was looking at,

00:56:32.260 --> 00:56:33.910
they talked about how they had to reshoot

00:56:33.910 --> 00:56:36.350
a couple of the epic battle scenes because

00:56:36.350 --> 00:56:39.433
a few of the guys had on
wristwatches and sunglasses.

00:56:40.500 --> 00:56:42.780
And they would like see it,

00:56:42.780 --> 00:56:44.350
and kind of seems like a myth

00:56:44.350 --> 00:56:46.570
because they could just
go in rotoscope it out.

00:56:46.570 --> 00:56:48.000
Those people deserve to be flogged.

00:56:48.000 --> 00:56:49.610
I mean at that point,
in front of everybody.

00:56:49.610 --> 00:56:51.230
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:56:51.230 --> 00:56:52.300
You will be killed.

00:56:52.300 --> 00:56:54.650
Pull their kilts up and
just give them a nice

00:56:55.520 --> 00:56:57.523
fanny flogging, butt flogging.

00:56:58.640 --> 00:57:01.300
Which brings me to my number four,

00:57:01.300 --> 00:57:05.780
I, again, "Braveheart"
was an honorable mention,

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:10.373
but I had to put
"Apocalypto" at number four.

00:57:11.660 --> 00:57:13.353
What an amazing Mel Gibson movie.

00:57:15.020 --> 00:57:16.540
Just kidding.
You're joking.

00:57:16.540 --> 00:57:18.015
Yeah, you remember that movie?

00:57:18.015 --> 00:57:18.848
I was gonna say.

00:57:18.848 --> 00:57:20.347
I remember Mel Gibson.
Yeah, it was good.

00:57:20.347 --> 00:57:21.890
It was decent.

00:57:21.890 --> 00:57:23.730
It was very violent.

00:57:23.730 --> 00:57:25.580
I mean, "Braveheart" is very violent.

00:57:26.450 --> 00:57:27.850
But anyway, that's not my number four.

00:57:27.850 --> 00:57:32.683
My number four, I know I'm
gonna surprise you here.

00:57:33.730 --> 00:57:36.080
I'm throwing another another
Coen Brothers movie up there,

00:57:36.080 --> 00:57:39.610
and now you know what it is,
"O Brother Where Art Thou,"

00:57:39.610 --> 00:57:43.713
went higher, two spots, than
"The Big Lebowski" for me.

00:57:44.690 --> 00:57:46.143
Released in 2000.

00:57:47.310 --> 00:57:49.550
I was a big Coen Brothers
fan by this point.

00:57:49.550 --> 00:57:51.460
So I was determined I was gonna see

00:57:51.460 --> 00:57:53.540
their next movie in theaters.

00:57:53.540 --> 00:57:57.583
And I remember just feeling,

00:57:59.480 --> 00:58:02.960
it was weird, I had this
connection to this movie

00:58:02.960 --> 00:58:04.170
the first time I saw it.

00:58:04.170 --> 00:58:06.780
Like, and I really think it had to do with

00:58:08.050 --> 00:58:11.793
the type of comedy, it was
like that old time-y comedy,

00:58:14.458 --> 00:58:19.123
and, the Southern humor,
and just the use of music.

00:58:21.480 --> 00:58:26.290
I really felt like it was a
movie that we would have made.

00:58:26.290 --> 00:58:27.897
I don't know, it was--
Yeah.

00:58:27.897 --> 00:58:32.740
I actually, I feel this was
a complete miss on my part,

00:58:32.740 --> 00:58:34.690
now that I'm thinking about it.

00:58:34.690 --> 00:58:37.320
Like I don't know why that,

00:58:37.320 --> 00:58:40.067
in all the lists that I
looked up to jog my memory,

00:58:40.067 --> 00:58:42.600
"O Brother" didn't come up.

00:58:42.600 --> 00:58:45.870
Well it didn't win a lot of awards.

00:58:45.870 --> 00:58:49.090
George Clooney won a Golden
Globe for best comedy actor,

00:58:49.090 --> 00:58:53.890
and I do think it won
best comedy or musical,

00:58:55.480 --> 00:58:58.887
as a, and it won the
Grammy album of the year.

00:58:58.887 --> 00:59:01.930
It won the freaking, the
soundtrack won album of the year

00:59:01.930 --> 00:59:03.608
because it was--

00:59:03.608 --> 00:59:04.441
The soundtrack was incredible.

00:59:04.441 --> 00:59:06.090
Amazingly curated by T-Bone Burnett.

00:59:06.090 --> 00:59:07.540
Every song.

00:59:07.540 --> 00:59:08.840
Every song was great.

00:59:08.840 --> 00:59:12.467
So yeah, the Southern comedy,

00:59:14.100 --> 00:59:18.210
the way that the history,
and the way that music

00:59:18.210 --> 00:59:22.110
was such a part, like,
at that point in my life

00:59:24.390 --> 00:59:26.990
like, I mean, music's so important to me,

00:59:26.990 --> 00:59:29.560
and there's been different
points when I thought

00:59:29.560 --> 00:59:31.730
I could be a music historian,

00:59:31.730 --> 00:59:33.070
not because I know a lot of stuff,

00:59:33.070 --> 00:59:37.530
but because I could see myself investing

00:59:37.530 --> 00:59:40.132
what it took to be that type of person.

00:59:40.132 --> 00:59:43.800
And I felt like that's, it
was kind of like watching,

00:59:43.800 --> 00:59:46.280
there was a documentary element to it.

00:59:46.280 --> 00:59:48.970
It felt like I was watching
a Ken Burns documentary

00:59:48.970 --> 00:59:53.490
on, you know, country music

00:59:55.260 --> 00:59:56.170
in some aspects.

00:59:56.170 --> 00:59:59.330
And so it resonated with
me on all those levels.

00:59:59.330 --> 01:00:01.330
And then the comedy of
it, and the characters,

01:00:01.330 --> 01:00:05.090
and it was just, and it was fun.

01:00:05.090 --> 01:00:05.923
You know?

01:00:05.923 --> 01:00:07.209
It wasn't too heavy.

01:00:07.209 --> 01:00:08.042
So it--

01:00:08.042 --> 01:00:11.150
Yeah like I said, I love it and,

01:00:11.150 --> 01:00:14.030
I don't know where I would put it,

01:00:14.030 --> 01:00:16.160
