Whereas you pretty much have
to be a teenager, or older,

01:02:05.860 --> 01:02:09.890
to really, I think, get into
"The Lord of the Rings."

01:02:09.890 --> 01:02:11.280
I didn't read the books

01:02:11.280 --> 01:02:12.780
until the movies started coming out,

01:02:12.780 --> 01:02:17.400
but I read each book
before the movie came out.

01:02:17.400 --> 01:02:19.420
Only time I've ever done that.

01:02:19.420 --> 01:02:21.410
Well "Game of Thrones"
I read all of the books

01:02:21.410 --> 01:02:23.200
before I watched any of that.

01:02:23.200 --> 01:02:26.340
And it only helped, especially--

01:02:26.340 --> 01:02:27.173
Yeah.

01:02:27.173 --> 01:02:29.780
Especially because they were so reverent

01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:32.253
of the books when they made the movies.

01:02:33.090 --> 01:02:36.770
And now, as I've already
recommended the extended versions.

01:02:36.770 --> 01:02:38.665
Yeah, it's the perfect,

01:02:38.665 --> 01:02:40.710
from a literary standpoint,

01:02:40.710 --> 01:02:43.030
it is the perfect fantasy series.

01:02:43.030 --> 01:02:44.643
It's been emulated.

01:02:46.560 --> 01:02:49.630
But it's never been
superseded, I don't think.

01:02:49.630 --> 01:02:54.630
And then, like you said,
the fidelity that the movie,

01:02:56.666 --> 01:02:59.883
the way that they translated
the books into the films.

01:03:01.720 --> 01:03:05.373
The level of detail,
the level of attention,

01:03:06.719 --> 01:03:10.320
it's just something that I don't think

01:03:10.320 --> 01:03:13.820
it had never happened before
and it hasn't happened since.

01:03:13.820 --> 01:03:17.693
No one's ever adapted
something with that much care.

01:03:19.216 --> 01:03:21.930
The way that you can watch
a superhero movie and

01:03:21.930 --> 01:03:25.430
I understand how someone might
feel like, you know what,

01:03:25.430 --> 01:03:28.240
it's just a little ridiculous,
it's a bit too much for me,

01:03:28.240 --> 01:03:29.750
I just can't get into it.

01:03:29.750 --> 01:03:34.750
I think you could say that
about "Lord of the Rings"

01:03:35.060 --> 01:03:38.100
if it wasn't "Lord of the
Rings" but the fact that it was,

01:03:38.100 --> 01:03:40.390
it is what it is, it has such a deep

01:03:42.420 --> 01:03:46.763
well of backstory and history,

01:03:48.350 --> 01:03:50.953
and a language and everything,

01:03:51.934 --> 01:03:56.934
it's easy to just immerse
yourself in that world and to,

01:03:57.150 --> 01:04:00.800
because you can't be, it's
really hard to be critical of it

01:04:00.800 --> 01:04:02.610
because it's done so well.

01:04:02.610 --> 01:04:05.350
Whereas if it's not you
can just roll your eyes,

01:04:05.350 --> 01:04:06.320
in that genre.

01:04:06.320 --> 01:04:07.810
That's what happened with Narnia,

01:04:07.810 --> 01:04:09.049
you know?
Right.

01:04:09.049 --> 01:04:11.740
Okay, so my number three.

01:04:11.740 --> 01:04:13.433
I've been on record,

01:04:13.433 --> 01:04:15.670
you shouldn't be surprised,

01:04:15.670 --> 01:04:16.600
I've been on record saying

01:04:16.600 --> 01:04:18.547
this is the best movie of all time.

01:04:18.547 --> 01:04:21.722
And it sounds like I'm
saying it as a joke.

01:04:21.722 --> 01:04:22.830
I can't believe it made number three.

01:04:22.830 --> 01:04:27.830
My number three movie of
all time, released in 2003,

01:04:28.490 --> 01:04:32.577
with no apologies from me is "Elf."

01:04:33.617 --> 01:04:38.617
"Elf" is one of, is my third
favorite movie of all time.

01:04:38.650 --> 01:04:41.020
You know, I didn't see it in the theater.

01:04:41.020 --> 01:04:44.260
It's sad that so many of my movies

01:04:44.260 --> 01:04:45.950
on my list I didn't see in the theater,

01:04:45.950 --> 01:04:47.500
but we watched it, of course

01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:49.070
we watch it every year Christmas.

01:04:49.070 --> 01:04:50.920
And every year at Christmas I'm like,

01:04:51.850 --> 01:04:54.360
I forget how good this movie is.

01:04:54.360 --> 01:04:56.070
One of these years I'm going to expect it

01:04:56.070 --> 01:04:58.490
to be as good as it actually is.

01:04:58.490 --> 01:05:02.970
Every scene, I mean, it taps
into the nostalgia of the,

01:05:02.970 --> 01:05:06.360
of those old stop motion movie,

01:05:06.360 --> 01:05:07.617
Christmas movies that we'd watch,

01:05:07.617 --> 01:05:09.720
"Rudolph the Red Nosed Reindeer"

01:05:09.720 --> 01:05:10.583
and "Frosty the Snowman."

01:05:10.583 --> 01:05:11.416
"Frosty the Snowman."

01:05:11.416 --> 01:05:15.061
It pays perfect homage to those.

01:05:15.061 --> 01:05:19.570
It's Will Ferrell in this
like super sweet mode,

01:05:19.570 --> 01:05:24.040
that's really, winsome.

01:05:24.040 --> 01:05:26.490
Every scene, it's like oh yeah,

01:05:26.490 --> 01:05:27.920
this one's, this is a really good scene,

01:05:27.920 --> 01:05:30.203
and this is a really good scene, and it's,

01:05:31.160 --> 01:05:32.920
the scenes build the movie.

01:05:32.920 --> 01:05:35.288
In some way, it's kind
of like, I know that,

01:05:35.288 --> 01:05:38.160
like a weird and maybe
obvious thing to say,

01:05:38.160 --> 01:05:39.950
but what I'm trying to get at is

01:05:40.820 --> 01:05:41.690
it's not just

01:05:45.080 --> 01:05:47.630
what, everything that
happens you feel like

01:05:47.630 --> 01:05:49.440
this is my favorite part of the movie.

01:05:49.440 --> 01:05:50.550
And then the next thing that happens,

01:05:50.550 --> 01:05:52.680
oh no this is my favorite
part of the movie.

01:05:52.680 --> 01:05:54.920
And you go through,

01:05:54.920 --> 01:05:58.390
there's only a few places where
that's not really the case.

01:05:58.390 --> 01:06:00.980
Like every scene has something

01:06:00.980 --> 01:06:02.700
that in that moment that you're watching

01:06:02.700 --> 01:06:05.010
it makes you think it's the best.

01:06:05.010 --> 01:06:07.820
It's like listening to The Hall and Oat's

01:06:07.820 --> 01:06:09.500
greatest hits album, when you're like,

01:06:09.500 --> 01:06:11.230
oh, this is their best song,

01:06:11.230 --> 01:06:12.930
and then the next one comes on.

01:06:12.930 --> 01:06:14.928
That's my experience in watching "Elf."

01:06:14.928 --> 01:06:17.880
And then there's, there's all
the memories of watching it

01:06:17.880 --> 01:06:20.500
with my family every year and
everybody's still loving it,

01:06:20.500 --> 01:06:21.880
nobody rolling their eyes.

01:06:21.880 --> 01:06:24.430
Okay, it's a great movie.

01:06:24.430 --> 01:06:28.120
And when we do top 10 favorite
Christmas movies of all time,

01:06:28.120 --> 01:06:29.463
it'll be my number one.

01:06:32.929 --> 01:06:34.160
Hey, you don't have to say anything,

01:06:34.160 --> 01:06:34.993
you don't have to say anything.

01:06:34.993 --> 01:06:37.493
No, no I mean, I think it's funny.

01:06:38.760 --> 01:06:40.570
It's a funny movie but it's funnier that

01:06:40.570 --> 01:06:42.070
it's your number three
favorite movie of all time.

01:06:42.070 --> 01:06:43.490
Jon Favreau wanted to,

01:06:43.490 --> 01:06:45.330
he was exploring making a sequel,

01:06:45.330 --> 01:06:47.390
but Will Ferrell didn't seem too into it.

01:06:47.390 --> 01:06:50.750
It's come up a few times over the decades,

01:06:50.750 --> 01:06:52.080
but it ain't gonna happen.

01:06:52.080 --> 01:06:53.920
I don't think it should happen.

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:55.920
I don't want it to happen.

01:06:55.920 --> 01:06:57.650
Okay, well I've already
told you my number two.

01:06:57.650 --> 01:06:59.520
I've already told you my number two.

01:06:59.520 --> 01:07:01.260
Which is "Lord of the Rings."

01:07:01.260 --> 01:07:02.546
Oh, so we're going to number one.

01:07:02.546 --> 01:07:03.379
So now we are both--

01:07:03.379 --> 01:07:04.610
Where we obviously agree.

01:07:04.610 --> 01:07:06.910
Yeah, we know what our
number one is, right?

01:07:08.050 --> 01:07:11.530
This is the thing that there was no,

01:07:11.530 --> 01:07:12.630
there was no question.

01:07:13.651 --> 01:07:16.147
I knew what I was going
to put in my number one,

01:07:16.147 --> 01:07:17.730
and that wasn't the difficult part,

01:07:17.730 --> 01:07:19.480
the difficult part was
filling the list out.

01:07:19.480 --> 01:07:21.330
But we'll say it on the count of three,

01:07:21.330 --> 01:07:22.500
and we'll say it at different times

01:07:22.500 --> 01:07:24.900
because of the delay
between our two video chats

01:07:24.900 --> 01:07:27.307
but three, two, one.

01:07:27.307 --> 01:07:29.547
"Pulp Fiction."
"Pulp Fiction."

01:07:29.547 --> 01:07:30.380
There it is.

01:07:30.380 --> 01:07:31.213
Released in 1994.

01:07:32.720 --> 01:07:36.877
Quentin Tarantino's follow
up to a "Reservoir Dogs."

01:07:37.960 --> 01:07:41.800
When I watched this movie, I went to,

01:07:41.800 --> 01:07:44.923
it's interesting, we didn't
watch this movie together.

01:07:44.923 --> 01:07:48.023
It was really gaining momentum.

01:07:48.950 --> 01:07:51.390
It had been out for a while.

01:07:51.390 --> 01:07:54.540
And I went to the Rialto theater,

01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:57.640
which is like in Raleigh
at like Five Points where--

01:07:57.640 --> 01:08:00.050
That's where, I saw it at the
Rialto for the first time,

01:08:00.050 --> 01:08:01.540
but did I see you because
you took me back to it?

01:08:01.540 --> 01:08:03.070
I think so.

01:08:03.070 --> 01:08:05.420
I know I didn't watch it
the first time with you.

01:08:05.420 --> 01:08:09.133
I think I watched it with Missy,
my girlfriend at the time.

01:08:10.157 --> 01:08:12.170
Because that was 19,
it might have been '95

01:08:12.170 --> 01:08:15.100
by the time we saw it, because
it took a while to get going

01:08:15.100 --> 01:08:16.620
and we were, I was,

01:08:16.620 --> 01:08:18.340
we were definitely dating at the time.

01:08:18.340 --> 01:08:21.560
But the funny thing is I don't
remember who I saw it with.

01:08:21.560 --> 01:08:26.280
I remember the theater, I
remember the theater had a stage.

01:08:26.280 --> 01:08:28.110
And it had these two big speakers

01:08:28.110 --> 01:08:31.090
just exposed and sitting out there

01:08:31.090 --> 01:08:33.150
below the screen, and it,

01:08:33.150 --> 01:08:34.720
it was a small theater,

01:08:34.720 --> 01:08:36.510
and you would just walk down the sides

01:08:36.510 --> 01:08:38.100
and then go into the middle,

01:08:38.100 --> 01:08:40.973
really old, rickety squeaky seats.

01:08:41.840 --> 01:08:44.050
And right, it just felt,

01:08:44.050 --> 01:08:47.540
it was the first time I'd
seen a movie at the Rialto,

01:08:47.540 --> 01:08:50.140
or any, like, art house theater.

01:08:50.140 --> 01:08:53.193
And I felt so out of place.

01:08:54.210 --> 01:08:57.460
Yet, so cool being there.

01:08:57.460 --> 01:08:59.950
You know, I was, being in high school

01:08:59.950 --> 01:09:03.250
and it was just like, there
were these hipster college kids

01:09:03.250 --> 01:09:06.370
and like these old guys wearing sweater,

01:09:06.370 --> 01:09:09.730
you know cardigans, like Professor types,

01:09:09.730 --> 01:09:12.143
and they were drinking
beer in their seats, and,

01:09:13.670 --> 01:09:15.940
it was, and then the movie started,

01:09:15.940 --> 01:09:19.310
and it started in in such a different way

01:09:19.310 --> 01:09:20.240
than any movie I'd seen.

01:09:20.240 --> 01:09:22.970
It had this opening title credit sequence,

01:09:22.970 --> 01:09:25.460
and I'm like, what is this?

01:09:25.460 --> 01:09:30.460
Yeah well he did, I
remember the feeling of,

01:09:32.310 --> 01:09:33.883
after I watched that movie,

01:09:34.790 --> 01:09:36.940
and this has happened a few times,

01:09:36.940 --> 01:09:39.750
where you just got to
sit there for a while.

01:09:39.750 --> 01:09:40.800
You can't get up.

01:09:40.800 --> 01:09:43.779
Well it was confusing because it,

01:09:43.779 --> 01:09:46.810
it was, you know, it
was circular narrative,

01:09:46.810 --> 01:09:48.210
it was presented out of order,

01:09:48.210 --> 01:09:49.320
and so like--

01:09:49.320 --> 01:09:50.500
Well, he was just,

01:09:50.500 --> 01:09:54.760
he was doing things that we
had never been exposed to.

01:09:54.760 --> 01:09:58.890
First of all, we hadn't really
seen anything that violent.

01:09:58.890 --> 01:10:00.538
You know?

01:10:00.538 --> 01:10:03.710
In your face, like, I
mean the type of violence.

01:10:03.710 --> 01:10:04.690
Gratuitous.

01:10:04.690 --> 01:10:07.530
Gratuitous, violence to
the point, and again,

01:10:07.530 --> 01:10:10.130
this is controversial,
Tarantino's controversial,

01:10:10.130 --> 01:10:11.530
there's gonna be people who are mad at us

01:10:11.530 --> 01:10:12.820
because we put this as the number one.

01:10:12.820 --> 01:10:14.190
But listen, this isn't,

01:10:14.190 --> 01:10:16.400
we're not trying to please
anybody with this list,

01:10:16.400 --> 01:10:17.810
we're just telling you what
our favorite movies are.

01:10:17.810 --> 01:10:20.160
And I know Tarantino is
a controversial figure.

01:10:21.470 --> 01:10:23.720
But the impact that movie had on me,

01:10:23.720 --> 01:10:28.720
and the fact that he
made extreme violence,

01:10:31.690 --> 01:10:34.316
funny, and I don't know,

01:10:34.316 --> 01:10:36.520
I know that that's that's problematic,

01:10:36.520 --> 01:10:38.150
and I find that problematic,

01:10:38.150 --> 01:10:40.450
and I wouldn't personally create it.

01:10:40.450 --> 01:10:45.123
But even, the scene where shoot Marvin,

01:10:46.510 --> 01:10:48.870
and it's actually the most horrific

01:10:48.870 --> 01:10:52.510
thing that you can imagine, but yet,

01:10:52.510 --> 01:10:54.850
incredibly funny at the same time.

01:10:54.850 --> 01:10:58.330
Tarantino had this way
of tapping into this

01:10:58.330 --> 01:11:01.220
sort of dark part of every person

01:11:01.220 --> 01:11:03.810
who wants to laugh at violence

01:11:03.810 --> 01:11:06.671
because they don't know how to compute it.

01:11:06.671 --> 01:11:08.110
Well, the thing that was funny was the,

01:11:08.110 --> 01:11:11.190
it was in those characters worlds

01:11:11.190 --> 01:11:13.610
it was just another day at the office.

01:11:13.610 --> 01:11:16.900
And so it was the most honest way

01:11:16.900 --> 01:11:20.594
for those characters to
react to that accident.

01:11:20.594 --> 01:11:22.450
And it was funny,

01:11:22.450 --> 01:11:24.730
like I mean, the dialogue.

01:11:24.730 --> 01:11:27.070
The dialogue is, it's unlike anything,

01:11:27.070 --> 01:11:29.110
we had never experienced that like,

01:11:29.110 --> 01:11:31.650
the royale with cheese
conversation that they have

01:11:31.650 --> 01:11:34.360
in the car in the first half of the movie,

01:11:34.360 --> 01:11:37.360
I remember us talking about
that and thinking like,

01:11:37.360 --> 01:11:38.980
no one's ever, no one's ever,

01:11:38.980 --> 01:11:41.010
we had never been exposed to anybody

01:11:41.010 --> 01:11:45.650
who would have this completely
tertiary conversation.

01:11:45.650 --> 01:11:47.050
We might have weird conversations.

01:11:47.050 --> 01:11:47.883
For extended.

01:11:47.883 --> 01:11:51.830
It felt like there was a
resonance in the fact that

01:11:52.670 --> 01:11:57.250
time was dedicated to two characters

01:11:57.250 --> 01:12:01.000
having this strange conversation.

01:12:01.000 --> 01:12:02.180
That's not moving anything along.

01:12:02.180 --> 01:12:05.020
And it really, except that it

01:12:05.020 --> 01:12:08.083
because they were walking
up and they were armed,

01:12:08.970 --> 01:12:10.750
you know, depending on what, you know,

01:12:10.750 --> 01:12:11.780
they were going to do something,

01:12:11.780 --> 01:12:13.460
all this tension was built, because--

01:12:13.460 --> 01:12:14.650
There's tension.

01:12:14.650 --> 01:12:16.360
What's happening?

01:12:16.360 --> 01:12:19.430
I find this extremely
funny, but it's also,

01:12:19.430 --> 01:12:22.603
I'm really tightening
up inside, and it was,

01:12:23.970 --> 01:12:27.680
and I was aware of the filmmaking process

01:12:27.680 --> 01:12:29.850
more so than I ever had been,

01:12:29.850 --> 01:12:33.720
maybe completely for
the first time that like

01:12:33.720 --> 01:12:37.880
a director's voice could move me.

01:12:39.760 --> 01:12:43.773
And it, so that was extremely inspiring

01:12:43.773 --> 01:12:46.890
to make a connection between the decisions

01:12:46.890 --> 01:12:48.510
that a director had made,

01:12:48.510 --> 01:12:52.140
and the experience that I was having.

01:12:52.140 --> 01:12:54.740
Yeah, and another part of that,

01:12:54.740 --> 01:12:57.140
that I think you'll agree with,

01:12:57.140 --> 01:13:00.853
is when we made movies, you know, like,

01:13:00.853 --> 01:13:03.930
when we made "Gutless
Wonders" or whatever,

01:13:03.930 --> 01:13:05.590
and some of the stuff
that we will start filming

01:13:05.590 --> 01:13:07.590
and writing at the same time growing up,

01:13:08.870 --> 01:13:13.380
we didn't think about film
scores as scoring, right?

01:13:13.380 --> 01:13:16.780
We didn't think about people making music

01:13:16.780 --> 01:13:20.380
that is intended to accompany.

01:13:20.380 --> 01:13:22.110
In our minds, the way
that we thought about

01:13:22.110 --> 01:13:23.540
film scores was soundtracks,

01:13:23.540 --> 01:13:27.470
we thought about songs that
were cool that had a vibe

01:13:27.470 --> 01:13:29.270
that you put into a
certain part of the movie

01:13:29.270 --> 01:13:32.800
and that's what Tarantino
did in that movie.

01:13:32.800 --> 01:13:37.800
That soundtrack introduced
me to genres of music,

01:13:39.220 --> 01:13:41.650
I mean that soundtrack
introduced me to Al Greene.

01:13:41.650 --> 01:13:42.483
Yeah.

01:13:42.483 --> 01:13:43.650
And from then on, it's like, oh,

01:13:43.650 --> 01:13:45.550
I'm an now Al Greene fan, you know?

01:13:45.550 --> 01:13:50.170
And every single song
was so carefully chosen.

01:13:50.170 --> 01:13:51.720
And I think that that resonated with us

01:13:51.720 --> 01:13:53.316
because the way that we think about music

01:13:53.316 --> 01:13:55.483
and the way we think about

01:13:56.491 --> 01:13:58.600
music being matched to
something on screen,

01:13:58.600 --> 01:14:01.190
and he was, he continues
to be a master at that,

01:14:01.190 --> 01:14:03.800
but that was something we,
even though it had been done,

01:14:03.800 --> 01:14:07.280
it hadn't been done in
nearly the same way.

01:14:07.280 --> 01:14:08.763
And the fact that, I mean.

01:14:11.690 --> 01:14:14.510
Basically if you, if you're
reading about cinema,

01:14:14.510 --> 01:14:15.840
and you're reading about this movie,

01:14:15.840 --> 01:14:18.130
you're gonna read about "Pulp Fiction"

01:14:18.130 --> 01:14:21.500
being a cultural watershed,
whether you like it or not.

01:14:21.500 --> 01:14:25.450
So it I think it was so
special to both of us

01:14:25.450 --> 01:14:28.563
and became our number one
because we experienced,

01:14:29.430 --> 01:14:32.960
personally, firsthand, in that moment,

01:14:32.960 --> 01:14:35.820
the cultural watershed, not just for film,

01:14:35.820 --> 01:14:38.333
but it impacted so much more,

01:14:39.870 --> 01:14:44.870
and to be at the age where that

01:14:46.760 --> 01:14:49.030
watershed could happen through,

01:14:49.030 --> 01:14:50.620
it can run through our body,

01:14:50.620 --> 01:14:53.580
just coursing through our
veins as we watched this thing,

01:14:53.580 --> 01:14:57.800
